export const ORK_SKILLS: Readonly<Record<string, string>> = {
  principles: "---\nname: principles\ndescription: Use when deciding how to split work into an Ork workflow - which steps are programs, which are Claude sessions, where a person decides - so it costs less than doing it by hand and can't go wrong quietly.\n---\n\n# How Ork splits work\n\nOrk exists to do long jobs for less than doing them by hand, and safely. Four\nrules decide the shape of a workflow.\n\n## 1. A program for what a program can do\n\nA model turn is for judgment. Anything a program can decide, it should: run the\ntests, read a pull request's state, count what changed, wait for CI.\n\n- **A tool node** (`tool.shell`, `tool.git`, `tool.gh`, `tool.aws`) or an\n  **`mcp.call`** does it with no session. With `output: { format: json }`, its\n  answer routes the run: `when: \"json.state == 'OPEN'\"`.\n- **`verify:`** on a session runs a check before its report counts, so a session\n  can't claim success past failing tests. **`before:`** hands it what a program\n  found, so it doesn't spend turns finding it.\n- **`watch:`** waits on CI or a review with a program that looks every few\n  seconds; hours of waiting cost nothing.\n- **`notify:`** lets a program tell a running session what it found, with no\n  model turn to carry the news.\n\n## 2. Spend where it pays\n\n- A small model and low effort for a narrow task (`model: haiku`,\n  `effort: low`); the large ones where thinking pays.\n- `budgetUsd` on each session and on the run (`limits.budgetUsd`). A loop of\n  sessions without a budget or `limits.timeout` gets a warning, for good reason.\n- Give a session what it needs in its `prompt` and `before:`, rather than\n  letting it search; and `isolation: readonly` for a reviewer that only reads.\n\n## 3. Make a mistake impossible before checking for it\n\n- Declare what a node says: `output.schema` lists on tool JSON and Claude\n  reports (`verdict: [approve, escalate]`). Conditions then compare against a\n  closed list, and a run fails a value off it rather than matching nothing.\n- A verdict is a declared field, never words to search a summary for.\n- Give each condition its opposite, and each failure a handler (`when: \"!ok\"`).\n- Check every draft with Ork's `check` tool until it's clean.\n\n## 4. A person decides what can't be undone\n\nMerging, deploying, publishing, spending a lot: put a `human.gate` before it,\nwith the safe option `recommended`. For a run nobody watches, give it a\n`timeout` and the safe `default`. You never answer a gate yourself.\n\n## An example\n\nA review that a program checks first, and a person signs off:\n\n```yaml\nschemaVersion: 1\nname: careful-review\ndescription: Lint with a program, review with a small model, and ask before merging.\ninputs:\n  pr:\n    type: string\n    required: true\nlimits:\n  budgetUsd: 3\nnodes:\n  lint:\n    type: tool.shell\n    command: pnpm lint\n    allow: [pnpm lint]\n  review:\n    type: claude.worker\n    prompt: \"Review pull request {{inputs.pr}}. The linter passed.\"\n    model: haiku\n    effort: low\n    isolation: readonly\n    budgetUsd: 1\n    output:\n      schema:\n        verdict: [approve, escalate]\n  ask:\n    type: human.gate\n    question: \"Merge pull request {{inputs.pr}}? The review says {{nodes.review.output.verdict}}.\"\n    options: [merge, wait]\n    recommended: wait\n  merge:\n    type: tool.gh\n    command: pr merge {{inputs.pr}} --squash\n    allow: [pr merge]\nedges:\n  - lint -> review\n  - review -> ask\n  - from: ask\n    to: merge\n    when: \"choice == 'merge'\"\n```\n\n`lint` costs nothing, `review` is a small model with a cap, and nothing merges\nwithout a person.\n",
  run: "---\nname: run\ndescription: Use when starting, following or explaining an Ork run - what it's doing, why it failed, what it cost, and what waits on the person - and when the person asks about their Ork runs.\n---\n\n# Running and following Ork workflows\n\nOrk runs workflows on the person's machine with their accounts, so a run spends\ntheir money and can push, merge or deploy. Start one only when they ask, and\nnever answer a decision for them.\n\n## Starting\n\n1. Find it: Ork's `workflows` tool gives each workflow's name and inputs.\n2. Check it first with `check` (`workflow: <name>`). Don't start one with an\n   error.\n3. Start it with `start_run`, giving every required input. Claude Code asks the\n   person first, unless they've allowed it. It answers with the run's id; the run goes on in the\n   background, whatever happens to this conversation.\n\nWithout Ork's tools: `ork run <workflow> --detach --input name=value`.\n\n## Following\n\n- `run` gives a run's status, what it waits on, its cost so far, and each\n  node's state. `runs` lists the newest runs.\n- `run_events` gives its newest events; `node` narrows them, and `last` or\n  `after` widens them. A `node.finished` event with `ok: false` says why a node\n  failed.\n- `explain_context` says what a node's session was given: its agent,\n  instructions, skills and files. Use it when a node didn't know something.\n- `usage` says what runs spent, by workflow and model, and how close the limits\n  are.\n\nSay what you found plainly: which node, what it said, and what it cost. Quote a\nnode's own words rather than retelling them.\n\n## What only the person does\n\n- **Decisions.** `decisions` lists the questions waiting on them: a gate, a loop\n  that ran out of rounds, a permission. Tell them what each asks, the options,\n  and the recommended one, and that they answer in `ork ui` or with\n  `ork decide` in their own terminal. Don't answer one, and don't run\n  `ork decide`; with Ork's plugin, a hook stops it.\n- **Stopping, pausing, resuming.** Tell them the command: `ork stop <run>`,\n  `ork pause <run>`, `ork continue <run>`, `ork resume <run>`. A node can be\n  stopped alone with `--node <id>`.\n\n## Without Ork's tools\n\n| Want                   | Command                        |\n| ---------------------- | ------------------------------ |\n| Runs, newest first     | `ork ps`                       |\n| A run's transcript     | `ork logs <run>` (`--follow`)  |\n| Its nodes and cost     | `ork tree <run>`               |\n| What runs spent        | `ork usage`                    |\n| What waits on a person | `ork decide --list`            |\n| A node's context       | `ork context explain <wf> <n>` |\n",
  write: "---\nname: write\ndescription: Use when writing or changing an Ork workflow (a `.ork/workflows/*.ork.yaml` file) - its nodes, edges, conditions and loops - and checking each draft until Ork accepts it.\n---\n\n# Writing an Ork workflow\n\nAn Ork workflow is one YAML file in the project, `.ork/workflows/<name>.ork.yaml`.\nIt names the nodes (Claude sessions, programs, a person's decisions) and the\nedges that carry work between them. Ork runs it on the person's machine with\ntheir own accounts.\n\n## How to work\n\n1. **Look first.** Ork's `workflows` tool lists the project's workflows; read\n   one that's close. `agents` lists the agents a node can use. Before you split\n   the work, read `/ork:principles`: a program where a program will do, a\n   session only for judgment.\n2. **Don't guess a field.** `workflow_format` gives the format; ask it for one\n   node type (`nodeType: \"human.gate\"`) to see that type's fields.\n3. **Check every draft** with Ork's `check` tool (`text`, or `workflow` for a\n   file), and fix what it says until it has no error. Read its warnings: they\n   mean something. Without Ork's tools, run `ork validate <file>`.\n4. **Then save the file**, beginning with the schema line below, so editors can\n   check it too.\n\n## The shape of a file\n\n```yaml\n# yaml-language-server: $schema=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mimshins/ork/main/packages/schema/workflow.schema.json\nschemaVersion: 1\nname: fix-and-ship\ndescription: Fix a failing test, check it with the test suite, and ask before opening a pull request.\ninputs:\n  issue:\n    type: string\n    required: true\n    description: What fails.\ndefaults:\n  model: sonnet\n  budgetUsd: 2\nlimits:\n  budgetUsd: 10\nnodes:\n  fix:\n    type: claude.worker\n    prompt: \"Fix this, and keep the change small: {{inputs.issue}}\"\n  tests:\n    type: tool.shell\n    command: pnpm test\n    allow: [pnpm test]\n  ask:\n    type: human.gate\n    question: \"Open a pull request for {{nodes.fix.output.branch}}?\"\n    options: [open, hold]\n    recommended: hold\n  pr:\n    type: tool.gh\n    command: pr create --fill --head {{nodes.fix.output.branch}}\n    allow: [pr create]\nedges:\n  - fix -> tests\n  - from: tests\n    to: fix\n    when: \"!ok\"\n    maxRounds: 2\n  - from: tests\n    to: ask\n    when: ok\n  - from: ask\n    to: pr\n    when: \"choice == 'open'\"\n```\n\nNode ids are lowercase: letters, digits, `-` and `_`. Secrets never go in the\nfile: a tool reads one with `env:` and `{{env.NAME}}`.\n\n## Nodes\n\n| Type                              | Does                                                                |\n| --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `claude.worker`                   | A Claude session that does one task and reports                     |\n| `claude.leader`                   | A session that plans and delegates to the nodes it owns (`parent:`) |\n| `claude.subagent`                 | A subagent inside its parent's session; it takes no edges           |\n| `tool.shell`                      | Any program, run without a shell; `allow:` lists command prefixes   |\n| `tool.git`, `tool.gh`, `tool.aws` | The person's own `git`, `gh`, `aws`, with an allow list             |\n| `mcp.call`                        | One tool call on an MCP server, with no session                     |\n| `human.gate`                      | A question with options and a recommended one                       |\n| `control.join`                    | Waits for its inputs: `all` (default), `any`, or `n` of them        |\n| `control.router`, `control.split` | Pass their input on; a router takes its first matching edge         |\n| `workflow.call`                   | Starts another workflow as its own run                              |\n\n- **A Claude node reports** `status` (`pass`, `changes_requested`, `done` or\n  `fail`), `summary`, `artifacts`, `branch`, and any field its `output.schema`\n  declares, such as `verdict: [approve, escalate]`. For another verdict, declare\n  a field: `status` can't take other values.\n- **A tool node reports** `exitCode`, `stdout`, `stderr`, and with\n  `output: { format: json, schema: { state: [OPEN, CLOSED] } }` its JSON as\n  `json`. Declare the fields you read: a run then checks them, and a typo can't\n  pass.\n- **A gate reports** `choice` and `answeredBy`. A `timeout` needs a `default`.\n\n## Edges and conditions\n\n- `a -> b`, `a -> b, c`, `b, c -> j` are plain edges, taken when the source\n  succeeded. A failed node takes no plain edge: handle a failure with\n  `when: \"!ok\"`, or `status == 'fail'` on a Claude node.\n- `{ from: a, to: b, when: \"...\" }` is taken when the condition holds. A bare\n  name reads the source's output: `when: \"verdict == 'approve'\"`. Quote any\n  condition that starts with `!`, or YAML reads it as a tag.\n- Conditions have `==`, `!=`, `<`, `>`, `&&`, `||`, `!`, and `any`, `all`,\n  `count`, `len`, `contains`. A join's `statuses` suits `any(statuses, 'fail')`.\n- A node runs when any edge into it is taken; only a join waits. A node with no\n  way in taken is skipped, and so is what follows it.\n- Templates, `{{inputs.x}}`, `{{nodes.a.output.field}}`, `{{run.id}}`, go in\n  `prompt`, `instructions`, `command`, `cwd` and `question`. Each value stays\n  one argument of a command, so it can't inject one.\n\n## Loops\n\nEvery cycle needs an edge with `maxRounds`. Give the way back a condition and\nthe way out its opposite, as `!ok` and `ok` above; a plain edge beside a loop\nfires every round. When a loop runs out, Ork asks a person what to do.\n\n## What `check` refuses, and why\n\n- **A value the source can't have** (`when.value`): `status == 'failed'` (it's\n  `fail`), a gate's `choice == 'aprove'` for `approve`, a value outside a\n  declared list. The message names the values it can have.\n- **A loop no condition ends**, a plain edge beside a loop, a loop of sessions\n  with no `limits.budgetUsd` or `limits.timeout`: warnings, each worth fixing.\n- **`no way the walk tried reaches x`**: Ork tried every way through, and none\n  got to `x`. A join waiting on a branch a first-match router never takes is\n  the usual cause.\n",
};
